ix}e^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the general solution of the system." 13194139535603 false] [17592186166452 1145 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{x}_c(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_c(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also exponential, i.e.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $a,b$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the derivative of $\\mathbf{x}_p$ with respect to $t$ gives\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute $\\mathbf{x}_p$ and $\\mathbf{x}'_p$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7e^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot e^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} + e^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n7\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}7a \\\\ 7b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3a + b \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}7a \\\\ 7b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3a + b - 1 \\\\ b - 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{3a + b - 1 = 7a; b - 3 = 7b\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nb - 7b = 3 \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad b = -\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $a$, we have\n\n$$\n3a - \\dfrac12 - 1 = 7a \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad a = -\\dfrac38\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAnd thus, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535603 false] [17592186166452 1145 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{x}_c(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_c(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068256/assets/c68bfa253f23fb2c116b8769744e6fd22b7b898047e3ef3fe97f66dd91fefaa1.png)\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also exponential, i.e.\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068256/assets/b270d56283fdced6971e1a00fc3e642e379fc9348a0e4fb17a97ba6db2d8fc13.png)\nwhere $a,b$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of $\\mathbf{x}_p$ with respect to $t$ gives\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068256/assets/ca497159ae72a8b87997c78815119bb3de53782878cb2209072dcc505c902f37.png)\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute $\\mathbf{x}_p$ and $\\mathbf{x}'_p$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 7[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}7a \\\\ 7b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3a+b \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}7a \\\\ 7b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3a+b-1 \\\\ b-3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3a+b-1=7a \\\\ b-3=7b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get \n\n$$\nb - 7b = 3\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\nb = -\\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n3a - \\dfrac12 - 1 = 7a\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\na = -\\dfrac38.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068256/assets/fff596d40155dc7787910405557a722b5258890e3fae88f60842de92fac47177.png)\n\nAnd thus, the general solution is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068256/assets/dc74c06c84905f8490932369e24cecd8f3b7eb64fdc4413e42c2f8bc1a3f3cbd.png)" 13194139535603 true] [17592186166453 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068256/assets/5b0bb8069abe99949fc52c0be82c22f5879d7ca0ac334af396f47efa41ae0b5e.png)\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068256/assets/f220005265275c9297b7beef58777203a94351cb4957a26ca630802664759dc5.png)" 13194139535603 true] [17592186166453 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535603 false] [17592186166453 1145 "We are told that the general solution to the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $c_1,c_2$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nNext, we find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$. Substituting $t = 0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nc_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{0} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\cdot e^{0} \\\\ e^{0}\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0^{2} - 0 + 4 \\\\ -0^{2} + 2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nc_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} + 4 \\\\ c_{2} + 2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{c_{1} + 4 = 6; c_{2} + 2 = 1\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $c_1 = 2$. From the second equation, we have $c_2 = -1$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535603 false] [17592186166453 1145 "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068256/assets/40f93d12ebe4519dc4aa15f0df4b568ffa25f25afd99ec45130b569083c21fe0.png)\nwhere $c_1,c_2$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2.$ Substituting $t = 0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{0} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\ {e}^{0}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-0+4 \\\\ -{0}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+4 \\\\ {c}_{2}+2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+4=6 \\\\ {c}_{2}+2=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $c_1 = 2.$ From the second equation, we have $c_2 = -1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068256/assets/948088eb5d495f3d8a8a96f038b5b036b53fa398544f9faedbb08026e96b661e.png)" 13194139535603 true] [17592186166454 1100 "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068256/assets/c9810bd07c9da4bd3d803740a85ce484cd92f4a0d8cb73af75d53602f8611a72.png)\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068256/assets/85139452af923a4f7b793fc47454cb99579ce0fa68ae33de3a15747501f624ef.png)\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as $\\mathbf{x}(t)=\\mathbf{x}_c(t)+\\mathbf{x}_p(t),$ where $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution $\\mathbf{x}_p(t).$" 13194139535603 true] [17592186166454 1100 "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cos (3t) \\\\ \\cos (3t) - \\sin (3t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as $\\mathbf{x}(t)=\\mathbf{x}_c(t)+\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$, where $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$." 13194139535603 false] [17592186166454 1145 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=\\mathbf{x}_c(t)+\\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nwhe